<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Territory of Nauru</h1><p class="st-meta">1920 CE – 1968 CE · capital Yaren</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A tiny phosphate island administered by Australia for the British Empire, whose mining stripped most of its land before independence in 1968.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> had attached Nauru to its <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/marshall-islands-protectorate">Marshall Islands Protectorate</a> in 1888, and rich phosphate deposits were found in 1900. Australian forces took the island in 1914, and in 1920 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> granted a mandate to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire</a>, which <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> shared, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> administering it.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phosphate">Phosphate</a> Commissioners mined the island to supply cheap fertiliser to Australian and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> farms, paying Nauruans small royalties. An administrator ruled with a council of chiefs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied Nauru from 1942 to 1945 and deported about 1,200 Nauruans to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chuuk">Chuuk</a>, where many died. After 1947 it was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> trust territory, and proposals to resettle the population on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/curtis-island">Curtis Island</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-queensland">Queensland</a> were rejected.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Led by Head Chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammer-deroburt">Hammer DeRoburt</a>, Nauru became independent on 31 January 1968, and soon bought the phosphate industry. A case at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a> over mining damage was settled by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Australia</a> in 1993.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1920 CE – 1968 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">North America and Oceania</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:97.91%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1920 CE – 1968 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yaren</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hammer DeRoburt</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism, Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Nauruan, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nauru">Nauru</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nauru</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1944&amp;focus=territory-of-nauru" aria-label="Territory of Nauru on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="9317 2197 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Territory of Nauru lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M9094 2680h0M8774 3709h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M9637 2453h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M9637 2453h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Nauru. <a href="/atlas?year=1944&amp;focus=territory-of-nauru">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
